{
  "dom_id": "step-q325981",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/7facf793b5ce6740a31d665d96b03dd591e7edc2a6c35e519fa3b805534e02c1.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$\n\n$t\\in [$\n$-\\pi ,$\n{{field-1}}$)\u22c3($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/80e49fe1187d1b9e42ef6e221568c9bafca1507238cc644afbda695f5cee2539.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{\u00a0}\\cup \\text{\u00a0}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$",
